WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.820
- In 1887, a young
physics teacher attached

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:05.250
a thin glass rod to the end of an arrow

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:07.710
and loaded it into his homemade crossbow.

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:10.440
He torched the rod's end
until a molten glass bead.

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:12.060
Roughly the size of a pin's head,

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:13.680
it was barely hanging on.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:15.735
And then he fired the arrow.

00:00:15.735 --> 00:00:17.996
(air whooshing)

00:00:17.996 --> 00:00:21.079
(suspenseful music)

00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:25.290
Bright sunlight streaming into the room

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:28.230
glinted off a tiny glass fiber.

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:30.060
At its thinnest, it was less than a tenth

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:32.490
of the diameter of a human hair.

00:00:32.490 --> 00:00:36.180
Now legally we cannot
shoot a crossbow indoors.

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:37.701
Luckily I am pretty quick.

00:00:37.701 --> 00:00:39.347
(Henry chuckles)

00:00:39.347 --> 00:00:42.430
(suspenseful music)

00:00:46.141 --> 00:00:47.353
That is cool.

00:00:47.353 --> 00:00:48.186
Look at that.

00:00:48.186 --> 00:00:49.830
Look how thin we got it.

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:51.630
The physics teacher was using these fibers

00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:53.280
to measure very small forces,

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:54.690
but in the decades that followed,

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:57.600
other researchers found
a different use for them:

00:00:57.600 --> 00:00:59.160
sending light signals.

00:00:59.160 --> 00:00:59.993
(quiet dramatic music)

00:00:59.993 --> 00:01:02.460
Glass fibers can carry
light even through bends.

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:04.590
Look at it. It's like that's 90 degrees.

00:01:04.590 --> 00:01:06.060
And during the 1960s,

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:07.710
the telecoms industry was desperate

00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:10.110
for a better way to
carry more information.

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:11.917
As one American engineer said,

00:01:11.917 --> 00:01:14.107
"It should be possible to transmit

00:01:14.107 --> 00:01:16.027
"all of the conversations going on

00:01:16.027 --> 00:01:18.517
"anywhere in the world simultaneously

00:01:18.517 --> 00:01:21.480
"over a single thread of light."

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:24.210
But these glass fibers were terrible.

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:26.880
The light faded so much that
you couldn't decode a signal

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:28.890
after just one kilometer.

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:30.870
No one thought they'd be practical.

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:32.411
Except for one man.

00:01:32.411 --> 00:01:33.244
(dramatic theatrical music)

00:01:33.244 --> 00:01:36.300
His own boss called his
dream "a wild goose chase,"

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:37.770
but if he was right,

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:40.620
if he could prove that fiber
optics were in fact viable,

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:42.420
and if someone could then
figure out how to make them

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:45.690
at scale, well, eventually
they could connect continents.

00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:47.070
How much is in this warehouse?

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:48.540
- Oh, there's millions of kilometers here

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:50.550
that we're pushing
through here every month.

00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:53.100
- It's approximately three times as hot

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:54.090
as the surface of the sun.

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:55.437
- That's crazy.

00:01:55.437 --> 00:01:56.850
And one day,

00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:59.399
it could build the internet.

00:01:59.399 --> 00:02:01.440
(dramatic music fading)

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:04.320
All that was unimaginable in the 1960s,

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:06.240
but even then telecom companies wanted

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:08.040
a better communication system,

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:10.050
just for phone calls.

00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:12.330
Between 1960 and 1973,

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:14.790
the number of daily
calls doubled in the U.S.

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:18.510
from 285 million to 576 million.

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:19.680
In that same time period,

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:21.420
NASA sent people to the moon

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:24.570
for a total cost of $26 billion.

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:27.150
While AT&amp;T had to spend
nearly triple that amount

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:29.640
just to install new
telephone infrastructure.

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:32.460
They just couldn't keep
up with the demand.

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:34.110
At the time, phone calls were mostly

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:36.090
transmitted over copper wires.

00:02:36.090 --> 00:02:38.730
Now imagine I'm trying
to phone my buddy Gregor.

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:40.500
Now, when I pick up, a constant current

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:41.940
immediately flows through the wire

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:43.890
to supply power to the phone.

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:45.420
When I speak, my voice vibrates

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:46.980
a tiny diaphragm in the mouthpiece

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:48.660
that modulates the current.

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:50.730
The louder I am, the
bigger the current swing,

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:52.080
and my voice's pitch changes

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:54.060
how fast the current oscillates.

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:55.620
At Gregor's end, that current reaches

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:58.170
a speaker that turns it back into sound.

00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:00.360
Now, this works for one call on one line,

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:02.850
but phone companies aren't
going to install wires

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:04.260
between every single house.

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:06.870
One cable needs to carry multiple calls.

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:09.720
So phone companies figure
out a neat little trick.

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:12.720
Even though humans can hear
frequencies up to 20 kilohertz,

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:15.168
you really only need to
hear the ones between

00:03:15.168 --> 00:03:16.950
300 and 3400 hertz

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:18.570
to understand what someone's saying.

00:03:18.570 --> 00:03:21.750
So phone companies drop
frequencies above four kilohertz

00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:23.760
or anything outside the voice band.

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:26.610
But if everyone used that
zero to four kilohertz band

00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:29.250
on the same line, well,
then all the conversations

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:30.510
would be on top of each other

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:31.410
and you wouldn't be able to hear

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:32.970
the person that you're talking to.

00:03:32.970 --> 00:03:35.520
Luckily, we can shift
those electrical signals,

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:38.070
so my conversation can
use zero to four kilohertz

00:03:38.070 --> 00:03:40.350
while another person's
can use four to eight

00:03:40.350 --> 00:03:43.320
and so on all the way
down the same copper wire.

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:45.180
At the other end, a filter separates out

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:46.470
the different frequency bands,

00:03:46.470 --> 00:03:49.350
similar to how a radio
can tune into one station.

00:03:49.350 --> 00:03:51.210
Then the signal is shifted back down

00:03:51.210 --> 00:03:52.830
to the zero to four kilohertz range

00:03:52.830 --> 00:03:54.000
before it hits the speaker,

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:56.340
so it sounds like a normal voice.

00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:57.870
But as phone companies started

00:03:57.870 --> 00:03:59.460
using higher and higher frequencies,

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:01.410
they started running into problems.

00:04:01.410 --> 00:04:03.390
That oscillating current
carrying the signal

00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:04.980
generates additional fields,

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:06.930
a magnetic field shown here in red,

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:09.240
and little loops of current
called eddy currents,

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:10.890
which are shown here in blue.

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:11.970
Now, because of their shape,

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:13.320
those loops resist changes

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:15.210
to the current at the center of the wire,

00:04:15.210 --> 00:04:17.910
and they reinforce
changes along the surface,

00:04:17.910 --> 00:04:19.440
but eddy currents get stronger

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:21.090
the faster the signal changes.

00:04:21.090 --> 00:04:22.260
And if you want more calls,

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:23.280
you need more bands,

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:25.260
which use higher and higher frequencies.

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:27.120
So soon the eddy currents
are strong enough

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:30.330
to cancel out the oscillation
in the center of the wire.

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:33.000
But if the signal can only
travel along the wire's surface,

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:35.250
it's limited to a smaller cross section.

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:37.680
So resistance goes up and
higher frequency signals

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:39.980
can't travel very far
before they're absorbed.

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:43.260
For a while, telephone companies
were able to work around

00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:46.500
the high loss by boosting
signals every few kilometers.

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:49.860
But past 65 megahertz, well,
the high frequencies died off

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:51.600
in less than two kilometers,

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:53.970
so they were too impractical to use.

00:04:53.970 --> 00:04:55.980
And this trade-off between signal power

00:04:55.980 --> 00:04:59.850
and data rate was actually
discovered back in 1948.

00:04:59.850 --> 00:05:02.670
The Shannon-Hartley theorem
described the maximum capacity

00:05:02.670 --> 00:05:04.290
of a communication channel based on

00:05:04.290 --> 00:05:06.540
the signal power, noise, and bandwidth,

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:07.800
or the difference between the highest

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:09.300
and lowest frequencies.

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:10.740
The best way to increase capacity

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:12.363
is to increase the bandwidth.

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:15.570
But they'd already reached
the limit for copper.

00:05:15.570 --> 00:05:18.220
The telecoms industry didn't
have another alternative,

00:05:18.220 --> 00:05:19.053
(dramatic music)

00:05:19.053 --> 00:05:20.550
but then they saw the light.

00:05:20.550 --> 00:05:23.970
Visible light has frequencies
in the hundreds of terahertz.

00:05:23.970 --> 00:05:25.867
As one researcher for Bell Labs put it,

00:05:25.867 --> 00:05:28.807
"the possibility of
extremely high frequencies

00:05:28.807 --> 00:05:31.837
"and accordingly vastly
wider bands of frequencies

00:05:31.837 --> 00:05:34.087
"appeared as a fabulous El Dorado,

00:05:34.087 --> 00:05:35.727
"always beckoning us onward."

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:38.550
But if you're going to use light,

00:05:38.550 --> 00:05:39.990
there are two problems.

00:05:39.990 --> 00:05:41.520
First, there wasn't a strong enough

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:43.050
light source to send a signal.

00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:45.150
A light bulb couldn't
produce enough power.

00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:47.250
There just weren't any options.

00:05:47.250 --> 00:05:48.870
- And along in 1960,

00:05:48.870 --> 00:05:50.850
Ted Maiman invented the laser.

00:05:50.850 --> 00:05:53.850
- [Reporter] A science fiction
weapon that melts steel,

00:05:53.850 --> 00:05:56.550
a death ray that could be more deadly

00:05:56.550 --> 00:05:57.870
than a nuclear bomb.

00:05:57.870 --> 00:05:59.850
It's a beam of light.

00:05:59.850 --> 00:06:02.460
This is called a laser.

00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:04.177
- [Henry] There's a running
joke that the laser was

00:06:04.177 --> 00:06:06.060
"a solution looking for a problem,"

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:08.219
but that's not really true.

00:06:08.219 --> 00:06:10.470
- [Donald] It rejuvenated the interest

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:13.470
in possible optical communications.

00:06:13.470 --> 00:06:15.217
- As one Bell Labs engineer said,

00:06:15.217 --> 00:06:16.947
"Think of all this bandwidth!"

00:06:18.090 --> 00:06:20.910
But they couldn't figure
out the second problem.

00:06:20.910 --> 00:06:23.400
Glass is not as clear as it looks.

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:26.520
Only a small amount of light
actually makes it through.

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:28.890
In fact, only a year after
the laser's invention,

00:06:28.890 --> 00:06:32.070
a Bell Labs scientist started
looking into fiber optics.

00:06:32.070 --> 00:06:34.170
His boss told him to check its loss,

00:06:34.170 --> 00:06:35.820
or how much a light signal weakens

00:06:35.820 --> 00:06:38.040
as it travels through the glass.

00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:40.260
Now, signal loss is
measured with decibels,

00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:42.450
similar to how you measure audio volume.

00:06:42.450 --> 00:06:44.670
Both are on a logarithmic scale,

00:06:44.670 --> 00:06:46.650
but while volume uses sound pressure,

00:06:46.650 --> 00:06:48.570
loss uses signal power.

00:06:48.570 --> 00:06:50.490
His boss said it would only be worthwhile

00:06:50.490 --> 00:06:53.220
if the loss was less than
20 decibels a kilometer,

00:06:53.220 --> 00:06:54.720
so it was comparable to copper.

00:06:54.720 --> 00:06:57.900
But when he checked the
loss for the best fibers,

00:06:57.900 --> 00:07:01.050
a thousand decibels a kilometer.

00:07:01.050 --> 00:07:02.970
To understand just how bad that was,

00:07:02.970 --> 00:07:05.220
imagine a leaky truck carrying sand

00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:06.870
over one kilometer.

00:07:06.870 --> 00:07:08.790
If the truck starts with 100 kilograms,

00:07:08.790 --> 00:07:10.320
and it ends up with one,

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:13.410
well, that represents a loss
of 20 decibels per kilometer.

00:07:13.410 --> 00:07:15.690
That's only 1% of the signal left.

00:07:15.690 --> 00:07:17.670
It's what was happening with copper,

00:07:17.670 --> 00:07:20.040
bad but still manageable.

00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:22.110
A loss of a thousand decibels a kilometer

00:07:22.110 --> 00:07:25.500
is like starting with the mass
of the observable universe

00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:29.130
and ending up with less than
the mass of a single atom.

00:07:29.130 --> 00:07:31.170
To be practical, these
fibers would need to improve

00:07:31.170 --> 00:07:33.810
by a factor of 10 the 98th.

00:07:33.810 --> 00:07:36.150
It sounded impossible. It would
have to be one of the most

00:07:36.150 --> 00:07:39.150
absurd engineering
improvements in history.

00:07:39.150 --> 00:07:40.770
So Bell Labs gave up.

00:07:40.770 --> 00:07:43.200
And fiber optics might
have died right there

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:44.943
if it wasn't for one man.

00:07:45.810 --> 00:07:47.220
Charles Kao.

00:07:47.220 --> 00:07:48.780
In 1960, he started working

00:07:48.780 --> 00:07:50.370
for an English telecoms lab

00:07:50.370 --> 00:07:51.930
that was looking for a way to communicate

00:07:51.930 --> 00:07:53.370
with higher frequencies,

00:07:53.370 --> 00:07:55.380
but not with fiber optics.

00:07:55.380 --> 00:07:57.630
Kao's boss said that it
was "a wild goose chase"

00:07:57.630 --> 00:08:00.000
and the rest of the lab agreed.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:01.770
But Kao kept coming back to it,

00:08:01.770 --> 00:08:04.477
asking, "Why is glass so lossy

00:08:04.477 --> 00:08:06.157
"and can we remove those factors

00:08:06.157 --> 00:08:08.850
"or are they intrinsic to the material?"

00:08:08.850 --> 00:08:09.720
If it was the latter,

00:08:09.720 --> 00:08:11.940
then fiber would be dead in the water.

00:08:11.940 --> 00:08:15.360
But if not, then maybe it had a chance.

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:16.470
So the question was:

00:08:16.470 --> 00:08:18.450
Why was the loss so high?

00:08:18.450 --> 00:08:20.250
Well, you might think it's because light

00:08:20.250 --> 00:08:22.680
is exiting through the
sides of this fiber,

00:08:22.680 --> 00:08:24.900
but glass is really
good at trapping light,

00:08:24.900 --> 00:08:26.430
even through bends.

00:08:26.430 --> 00:08:27.420
Here's how.

00:08:27.420 --> 00:08:29.310
I've got a bucket of propylene glycol here

00:08:29.310 --> 00:08:30.480
and a green laser.

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:32.240
When I remove the tape,

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:36.090
(gentle dramatic music)

00:08:36.090 --> 00:08:39.540
the laser bounces one, two, three times.

00:08:39.540 --> 00:08:41.220
And there are two things
working together here

00:08:41.220 --> 00:08:43.350
that actually trap the light.

00:08:43.350 --> 00:08:46.320
See, light travels slower in
some materials than others,

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:49.500
and it travels as a wave
shown here as a line of crests

00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:52.650
with the beam always pointing
perpendicular to that line.

00:08:52.650 --> 00:08:54.750
When the wave meets a
boundary at an angle,

00:08:54.750 --> 00:08:58.200
one end of the crest crosses
into the new material first,

00:08:58.200 --> 00:09:00.480
and it changes speed before the rest.

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:02.670
If light travels slower
in that new material,

00:09:02.670 --> 00:09:05.820
then that end drags
behind and the beam bends

00:09:05.820 --> 00:09:08.340
or refracts away from the boundary.

00:09:08.340 --> 00:09:11.070
How much it bends depends on
the angle it hits the boundary

00:09:11.070 --> 00:09:13.440
and on how much each
material slows light down,

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:16.110
which is described by
their index of refraction.

00:09:16.110 --> 00:09:18.333
This relationship is
defined by Snell's Law.

00:09:19.650 --> 00:09:21.600
Now if light is moving from a material

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:23.310
with a higher index of refraction

00:09:23.310 --> 00:09:25.020
into one with a lower index,

00:09:25.020 --> 00:09:27.240
like propylene glycol to air,

00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:29.670
well then one part of
the wave crest speeds up

00:09:29.670 --> 00:09:31.890
and the beam bends toward the boundary.

00:09:31.890 --> 00:09:33.990
But as you increase
the angle of the light,

00:09:33.990 --> 00:09:37.530
the refracted beam gets closer
and closer to the boundary

00:09:37.530 --> 00:09:39.420
until it's running right along it.

00:09:39.420 --> 00:09:41.580
That's the critical angle.

00:09:41.580 --> 00:09:43.470
A little bit more and all the light

00:09:43.470 --> 00:09:45.600
reflects back into the glycol.

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:47.280
That's total internal reflection,

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:48.810
and you get the exact critical angle

00:09:48.810 --> 00:09:51.270
by setting the angle of
refraction to 90 degrees

00:09:51.270 --> 00:09:53.280
and then rearranging Snell's Law.

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:56.190
- The key of it is the
bigger the difference

00:09:56.190 --> 00:09:58.050
in index of refraction,

00:09:58.050 --> 00:10:00.270
the more powerful this effect is.

00:10:00.270 --> 00:10:03.570
If I have a really subtle
difference between these two,

00:10:03.570 --> 00:10:08.570
then the amount of bending that
takes place will be smaller.

00:10:08.790 --> 00:10:10.320
- You can do the same demo with water,

00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:12.180
but propylene glycol works a bit better

00:10:12.180 --> 00:10:14.130
because it has a higher refractive index,

00:10:14.130 --> 00:10:16.601
1.43 compared with 1.33.

00:10:16.601 --> 00:10:18.420
(liquid splashing) See, once
the liquid is low enough

00:10:18.420 --> 00:10:20.490
for the angle of the
stream to be much sharper,

00:10:20.490 --> 00:10:22.350
the light breaks through the water stream,

00:10:22.350 --> 00:10:25.290
but it stays trapped in
the propylene glycol.

00:10:25.290 --> 00:10:26.340
And if you use something with

00:10:26.340 --> 00:10:28.080
an even higher refractive index,

00:10:28.080 --> 00:10:30.330
like the glass types
Kao was investigating,

00:10:30.330 --> 00:10:32.670
well, then the critical
angle decreases even more,

00:10:32.670 --> 00:10:35.520
which traps the light signals
through even sharper turns.

00:10:36.420 --> 00:10:38.010
Okay, but the light has to move from

00:10:38.010 --> 00:10:40.500
a high refractive index to a low one.

00:10:40.500 --> 00:10:42.150
In air that works right up

00:10:42.150 --> 00:10:44.310
until something touches the glass.

00:10:44.310 --> 00:10:45.810
If you disturb that boundary,

00:10:45.810 --> 00:10:48.210
well, light can leak out the side.

00:10:48.210 --> 00:10:50.040
So about a decade before Kao,

00:10:50.040 --> 00:10:51.600
researchers realized that a fiber

00:10:51.600 --> 00:10:53.850
needed to have two types of glass.

00:10:53.850 --> 00:10:56.970
The middle, the core, has
the higher refractive index,

00:10:56.970 --> 00:10:59.550
and around it goes a
second glass, the cladding,

00:10:59.550 --> 00:11:01.500
with a lower refractive index.

00:11:01.500 --> 00:11:03.900
Together, they keep the light trapped.

00:11:03.900 --> 00:11:06.600
Now the fiber could be
touched, coated in plastic,

00:11:06.600 --> 00:11:08.700
buried in a cable, bent around things,

00:11:08.700 --> 00:11:10.890
and the light would still
travel down the core

00:11:10.890 --> 00:11:12.554
and come out the other end.

00:11:12.554 --> 00:11:13.387
(quiet dramatic music)

00:11:13.387 --> 00:11:14.760
So Kao knew that if the high loss

00:11:14.760 --> 00:11:16.470
wasn't from light leaking out the sides,

00:11:16.470 --> 00:11:18.420
well, it had to be from something else.

00:11:18.420 --> 00:11:20.850
He spoke with material
scientists and glass experts,

00:11:20.850 --> 00:11:23.550
but no one seemed to know
what was really causing it.

00:11:23.550 --> 00:11:25.590
He kept looking and eventually he landed

00:11:25.590 --> 00:11:27.090
on two potential sources:

00:11:27.090 --> 00:11:28.533
scattering and absorption.

00:11:29.700 --> 00:11:30.690
If you shine light through

00:11:30.690 --> 00:11:32.490
a perfect crystal that's transparent,

00:11:32.490 --> 00:11:34.230
there's going to be a
little bit of reflection,

00:11:34.230 --> 00:11:36.420
but almost all of the
electromagnetic energy

00:11:36.420 --> 00:11:39.270
will keep going down in the
same direction that it started.

00:11:39.270 --> 00:11:41.730
But glass isn't a crystalline solid.

00:11:41.730 --> 00:11:45.180
The basic building block is
silica or silicon dioxide,

00:11:45.180 --> 00:11:48.060
one silicon atom connected
to four oxygen atoms

00:11:48.060 --> 00:11:50.940
in a tetrahedra or pyramid shape.

00:11:50.940 --> 00:11:53.370
In the glass, the pyramids
are connected at the corners,

00:11:53.370 --> 00:11:56.100
so every oxygen is shared
with another silicon.

00:11:56.100 --> 00:11:58.170
That's why it's called silicon dioxide.

00:11:58.170 --> 00:11:59.220
For every two oxygens,

00:11:59.220 --> 00:12:01.350
there's one silicon atom.

00:12:01.350 --> 00:12:03.000
This is an amorphous solid.

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:05.700
Unlike a crystal, its atoms
have no long-range order.

00:12:05.700 --> 00:12:08.430
Instead, its disordered
liquid-like structure

00:12:08.430 --> 00:12:10.590
locks in as it cools,

00:12:10.590 --> 00:12:11.760
with the exact arrangement

00:12:11.760 --> 00:12:14.610
depending on the glass
type and cooling speed.

00:12:14.610 --> 00:12:17.010
For example, if you quench
the glass really fast,

00:12:17.010 --> 00:12:19.650
the structure locks at
a higher temperature.

00:12:19.650 --> 00:12:21.960
The final solid has lots of dense regions,

00:12:21.960 --> 00:12:24.540
which re-radiate more
light than the sparse ones.

00:12:24.540 --> 00:12:26.820
The light scatters,
which weakens the signal.

00:12:26.820 --> 00:12:28.620
This is called Rayleigh scattering,

00:12:28.620 --> 00:12:30.690
and you can't get rid of it completely,

00:12:30.690 --> 00:12:32.520
but if you cool the glass slowly,

00:12:32.520 --> 00:12:34.380
those pyramids have more time to settle

00:12:34.380 --> 00:12:35.820
and pack together evenly.

00:12:35.820 --> 00:12:38.490
The structure locks in
at a lower temperature

00:12:38.490 --> 00:12:39.813
and light scatters less.

00:12:40.980 --> 00:12:43.470
But Kao needed to know
the minimum scattering.

00:12:43.470 --> 00:12:45.240
Was it 10 decibels a kilometer,

00:12:45.240 --> 00:12:46.860
a hundred, a thousand?

00:12:46.860 --> 00:12:49.230
He needed something concrete.

00:12:49.230 --> 00:12:51.840
Finally, he stumbled on
a paper by Robert Maurer,

00:12:51.840 --> 00:12:53.490
a scientist from Corning Glass

00:12:53.490 --> 00:12:54.840
who measured the Rayleigh scattering

00:12:54.840 --> 00:12:57.213
across several glass
types and cooling rates.

00:12:58.110 --> 00:13:00.180
Kao used his calculations to estimate

00:13:00.180 --> 00:13:02.280
that if the glass's structure was locked

00:13:02.280 --> 00:13:04.200
at a thousand degrees Celsius,

00:13:04.200 --> 00:13:05.490
well, its scattering loss would

00:13:05.490 --> 00:13:08.430
be around one decibel a kilometer.

00:13:08.430 --> 00:13:09.510
He was surprised.

00:13:09.510 --> 00:13:10.830
He expected scattering loss

00:13:10.830 --> 00:13:12.930
to increase with higher temperatures.

00:13:12.930 --> 00:13:15.570
That's what causes more
density fluctuations,

00:13:15.570 --> 00:13:18.270
but a thousand degrees
Celsius was pretty hot

00:13:18.270 --> 00:13:20.130
compared to other glass types,

00:13:20.130 --> 00:13:22.020
so then scattering couldn't be behind

00:13:22.020 --> 00:13:23.520
those crazy high losses.

00:13:23.520 --> 00:13:25.590
Absorption had to be the problem,

00:13:25.590 --> 00:13:27.300
but Kao didn't know what exactly

00:13:27.300 --> 00:13:29.010
was absorbing so much light.

00:13:29.010 --> 00:13:31.893
Was it the glass itself or something else?

00:13:32.730 --> 00:13:34.410
Well, absorption works like this.

00:13:34.410 --> 00:13:37.500
In a single atom, electrons
sit at discrete energy levels,

00:13:37.500 --> 00:13:40.320
and there are usually some
empty spaces in the upper ones,

00:13:40.320 --> 00:13:42.150
but pack atoms together into a solid

00:13:42.150 --> 00:13:43.620
and those levels smear up

00:13:43.620 --> 00:13:46.320
into wide bands separated by gaps.

00:13:46.320 --> 00:13:47.550
Now when light hits the surface,

00:13:47.550 --> 00:13:50.100
it can excite an electron
to a higher band,

00:13:50.100 --> 00:13:51.990
but only if the photon has at least

00:13:51.990 --> 00:13:54.210
as much energy as this band gap.

00:13:54.210 --> 00:13:56.100
If it does, the photon is absorbed

00:13:56.100 --> 00:13:58.680
and eventually it releases
that energy as heat.

00:13:58.680 --> 00:13:59.790
In something like silicon,

00:13:59.790 --> 00:14:01.500
that band gap is very small.

00:14:01.500 --> 00:14:04.320
It's only around 1.12 electron volts.

00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:07.620
Visible light is between 1.63 to 3.26.

00:14:07.620 --> 00:14:09.000
It's got plenty of energy,

00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:10.650
so it gets absorbed.

00:14:10.650 --> 00:14:13.050
That's why pure silicon
looks opaque to us,

00:14:13.050 --> 00:14:15.900
but the silicon in glass is
connected to oxygen atoms

00:14:15.900 --> 00:14:18.600
that pull more on the shared electrons.

00:14:18.600 --> 00:14:21.120
Now it takes a lot more
energy to excite an electron.

00:14:21.120 --> 00:14:24.690
The band gap is much larger,
around nine electron volts.

00:14:24.690 --> 00:14:26.160
Visible light doesn't have enough energy,

00:14:26.160 --> 00:14:28.140
so it passes right through,

00:14:28.140 --> 00:14:30.840
and that's why glass looked clear to us.

00:14:30.840 --> 00:14:33.240
But most glass types aren't pure silica.

00:14:33.240 --> 00:14:35.010
The raw materials for glass,

00:14:35.010 --> 00:14:38.190
from sand, soda, lime,
and other ingredients,

00:14:38.190 --> 00:14:40.980
well, they contain
impurities like trace metals,

00:14:40.980 --> 00:14:43.830
and these metals absorbed far
more light than silica did.

00:14:44.670 --> 00:14:47.490
One of the worst impurities
in glass is iron.

00:14:47.490 --> 00:14:48.810
Inside the glass structure,

00:14:48.810 --> 00:14:50.910
iron atoms form positive ions

00:14:50.910 --> 00:14:52.830
that strongly absorb violet, bluish,

00:14:52.830 --> 00:14:54.870
and infrared wavelengths.

00:14:54.870 --> 00:14:56.430
Of the visible light spectrum,

00:14:56.430 --> 00:14:59.217
impure glass doesn't
absorb green light as much,

00:14:59.217 --> 00:15:02.340
and you can even see this
in a regular glass pane.

00:15:02.340 --> 00:15:03.600
Looking at it from the edge,

00:15:03.600 --> 00:15:06.150
light travels down the
entire width of the glass,

00:15:06.150 --> 00:15:07.920
so by the time it reaches your eye,

00:15:07.920 --> 00:15:09.930
only green light is left.

00:15:09.930 --> 00:15:11.550
So if you can see this attenuation

00:15:11.550 --> 00:15:13.050
after just a few centimeters,

00:15:13.050 --> 00:15:15.300
imagine a glass fiber
trying to carry light

00:15:15.300 --> 00:15:17.730
for kilometers and kilometers.

00:15:17.730 --> 00:15:19.293
No wonder it had a crazy loss.

00:15:20.160 --> 00:15:21.600
Kao spoke with more experts

00:15:21.600 --> 00:15:23.640
who estimated that
without those metal ions,

00:15:23.640 --> 00:15:26.400
the loss would drop to around
20 decibels a kilometer,

00:15:26.400 --> 00:15:27.510
comparable to copper,

00:15:27.510 --> 00:15:29.379
just by removing impurities.

00:15:29.379 --> 00:15:30.212
(quiet dramatic music)

00:15:30.212 --> 00:15:31.800
It was all theoretical at this point,

00:15:31.800 --> 00:15:33.660
but it was enough for Kao.

00:15:33.660 --> 00:15:36.690
In 1966, his team published
a paper on their theories,

00:15:36.690 --> 00:15:39.547
concluding with "the crucial
material problem appears

00:15:39.547 --> 00:15:42.960
"to be one which is difficult
but not impossible."

00:15:42.960 --> 00:15:45.150
But nobody really paid
attention to his paper

00:15:45.150 --> 00:15:48.330
because it really seemed impossible.

00:15:48.330 --> 00:15:52.200
A 10 to the 98th times
improvement was unprecedented,

00:15:52.200 --> 00:15:55.380
and Kao didn't actually
measure the loss in glass.

00:15:55.380 --> 00:15:57.540
He didn't have anything concrete,

00:15:57.540 --> 00:15:59.133
so he needed proof.

00:16:00.330 --> 00:16:02.550
Kao was trying to invent
a new way to communicate,

00:16:02.550 --> 00:16:05.640
but even today actually
staying connected can be hard.

00:16:05.640 --> 00:16:06.960
In fact, we just traveled to Spain

00:16:06.960 --> 00:16:08.490
for the eclipse a few weeks ago,

00:16:08.490 --> 00:16:10.650
