00:17:25.380 --> 00:17:26.965
Now, let's go back to Kao.

00:17:26.965 --> 00:17:27.798
(striking dramatic music)

00:17:27.798 --> 00:17:29.460
Kao needed to prove that his theoretical

00:17:29.460 --> 00:17:31.560
low-loss glass was really possible,

00:17:31.560 --> 00:17:33.570
so he decided to measure the purest glass

00:17:33.570 --> 00:17:36.141
anyone had ever made, fused silica.

00:17:36.141 --> 00:17:37.740
Now, the reason glass manufacturers

00:17:37.740 --> 00:17:39.450
had added all those extra ingredients

00:17:39.450 --> 00:17:41.400
was to bring sand's melting point down

00:17:41.400 --> 00:17:43.920
to around 1400 degrees Celsius,

00:17:43.920 --> 00:17:46.590
but fused silica was pure silicon dioxide

00:17:46.590 --> 00:17:49.290
melted at over 2,000 degrees Celsius,

00:17:49.290 --> 00:17:51.570
so it was a lot harder to make,

00:17:51.570 --> 00:17:53.430
and that meant that Kao
couldn't get his hands

00:17:53.430 --> 00:17:54.660
on much of it.

00:17:54.660 --> 00:17:56.010
Measuring loss normally takes

00:17:56.010 --> 00:17:57.510
hundreds of meters of cable,

00:17:57.510 --> 00:17:59.190
if not kilometers,

00:17:59.190 --> 00:18:02.730
but he only had pieces less
than 30 centimeters long.

00:18:02.730 --> 00:18:04.560
To measure the loss in
something that small,

00:18:04.560 --> 00:18:05.760
he and his colleagues invented

00:18:05.760 --> 00:18:08.280
a new, very precise instrument.

00:18:08.280 --> 00:18:10.800
Kao spent the next three
years working on it,

00:18:10.800 --> 00:18:12.660
but when he finally measured a sample,

00:18:12.660 --> 00:18:16.281
he found that fused silica had zero loss.

00:18:16.281 --> 00:18:17.114
(dramatic music continues)

00:18:17.114 --> 00:18:18.570
That couldn't be right.

00:18:18.570 --> 00:18:21.300
In a panic, Kao ran his experiment again.

00:18:21.300 --> 00:18:23.700
It turned out that the loss was so low

00:18:23.700 --> 00:18:25.680
that it was within their margin of error,

00:18:25.680 --> 00:18:29.040
a mere plus or minus four
decibels a kilometer.

00:18:29.040 --> 00:18:30.870
He spent months fine-tuning his equipment,

00:18:30.870 --> 00:18:34.590
and in 1969, he finally
reported that fused silica

00:18:34.590 --> 00:18:38.250
had a loss of less than
five decibels a kilometer.

00:18:38.250 --> 00:18:40.200
Now, that got everyone's attention.

00:18:40.200 --> 00:18:41.942
One scientist at Bell Labs said,

00:18:41.942 --> 00:18:43.357
(audience applauding)
"Kao gave everyone a jolt.

00:18:43.357 --> 00:18:44.887
"That was the first practical measurement

00:18:44.887 --> 00:18:48.000
"which said, 'Hey, you're
not just whistling Dixie.'"

00:18:48.000 --> 00:18:49.320
Now everyone was interested.

00:18:49.320 --> 00:18:51.060
Telecom labs around the planet

00:18:51.060 --> 00:18:53.010
rushed to fund fiber optics groups,

00:18:53.010 --> 00:18:55.560
but Kao had only worked
with small samples.

00:18:55.560 --> 00:18:57.150
Nobody knew whether kilometers

00:18:57.150 --> 00:18:59.880
of low-loss fiber were even possible.

00:18:59.880 --> 00:19:02.370
The problem caught the
attention of one Robert Maurer,

00:19:02.370 --> 00:19:04.740
Corning researcher and author
of the scattering paper

00:19:04.740 --> 00:19:06.660
that Kao had referenced.

00:19:06.660 --> 00:19:07.860
To make a practical fiber,

00:19:07.860 --> 00:19:10.200
he saw three major problems to tackle.

00:19:10.200 --> 00:19:12.720
First, he needed to
decide which glass to use.

00:19:12.720 --> 00:19:15.480
He could try purifying raw
materials for normal glass,

00:19:15.480 --> 00:19:17.460
or he could try using fused silica,

00:19:17.460 --> 00:19:19.890
which Kao had already
proven to be very pure.

00:19:19.890 --> 00:19:21.930
Most labs were avoiding fused silica

00:19:21.930 --> 00:19:24.270
because of how hard it was to work with.

00:19:24.270 --> 00:19:26.493
But luckily, Corning
had a secret technique.

00:19:27.480 --> 00:19:29.610
Back in the 1930s,
another Corning scientist

00:19:29.610 --> 00:19:33.690
had ran silicon tetrachloride
gas through an oxygen flame.

00:19:33.690 --> 00:19:35.520
In the heat, water vapor from the flame

00:19:35.520 --> 00:19:37.170
would break silicon tetrachloride

00:19:37.170 --> 00:19:40.440
into hydrogen chloride
gas and silicon dioxide.

00:19:40.440 --> 00:19:42.270
The latter would then
start clicking together

00:19:42.270 --> 00:19:44.760
in a fine glassy silica powder.

00:19:44.760 --> 00:19:46.530
Then the powder would
be pressed into shape

00:19:46.530 --> 00:19:49.890
and melted to make fused silica.

00:19:49.890 --> 00:19:51.390
Corning was one of the few companies

00:19:51.390 --> 00:19:52.890
in the world using this technique

00:19:52.890 --> 00:19:55.563
called flame hydrolysis
to make ultra-pure glass.

00:19:56.790 --> 00:19:58.230
So they had the supply,

00:19:58.230 --> 00:20:01.080
but there was a bigger
problem with fused silica.

00:20:01.080 --> 00:20:03.870
It has a refractive index of 1.46,

00:20:03.870 --> 00:20:05.670
which is the lowest of any glass,

00:20:05.670 --> 00:20:07.950
but for total internal reflection to work,

00:20:07.950 --> 00:20:10.350
the core needs to have a
higher refractive index

00:20:10.350 --> 00:20:11.580
than the cladding.

00:20:11.580 --> 00:20:14.370
So if Maurer wanted to
use a fused silica core,

00:20:14.370 --> 00:20:15.960
well then finding another glass

00:20:15.960 --> 00:20:18.480
with a lower refractive
index for the cladding,

00:20:18.480 --> 00:20:19.680
well, that'd be a nightmare.

00:20:19.680 --> 00:20:21.960
It simply didn't exist.

00:20:21.960 --> 00:20:25.650
So everyone abandoned fused
silica except for Maurer.

00:20:25.650 --> 00:20:27.967
He said, "I had no reason
to think fused silica

00:20:27.967 --> 00:20:30.667
"was any better, but nobody
else was working on it.

00:20:30.667 --> 00:20:31.957
"If you do something that's different

00:20:31.957 --> 00:20:33.127
"from what everybody else is doing,

00:20:33.127 --> 00:20:34.567
"you've got two advantages.

00:20:34.567 --> 00:20:37.477
"One is you may succeed
where they fail, of course,

00:20:37.477 --> 00:20:39.727
"but even if you fail, you
will gather information

00:20:39.727 --> 00:20:41.640
"that they don't gather."

00:20:41.640 --> 00:20:42.900
All the other labs were thinking

00:20:42.900 --> 00:20:44.610
of using a fused silica core.

00:20:44.610 --> 00:20:46.680
After all, that's where the light travels,

00:20:46.680 --> 00:20:50.439
but Maurer decided to use
fused silica for the cladding.

00:20:50.439 --> 00:20:52.770
Then he could easily raise the
refractive index of the core

00:20:52.770 --> 00:20:55.830
by adding some impurities or dopants.

00:20:55.830 --> 00:20:58.320
- The only glass that we had

00:20:58.320 --> 00:21:00.690
with a slightly higher
index than fused silica

00:21:00.690 --> 00:21:03.750
was this titanium-doped glass.

00:21:03.750 --> 00:21:05.520
- [Henry] But that would
mean adding titanium

00:21:05.520 --> 00:21:07.270
right where light needed to travel.

00:21:08.220 --> 00:21:11.190
Kao worked hard to prove that
glass could be pure enough.

00:21:11.190 --> 00:21:13.770
All the other labs were trying
to get rid of impurities,

00:21:13.770 --> 00:21:16.113
and Maurer wanted to add some back in.

00:21:17.010 --> 00:21:19.050
But he wasn't completely crazy.

00:21:19.050 --> 00:21:20.880
Like silicon, the titanium in glass

00:21:20.880 --> 00:21:23.970
can easily share electrons
with four oxygen atoms,

00:21:23.970 --> 00:21:25.770
forming the same pyramid shape,

00:21:25.770 --> 00:21:28.698
so it wouldn't disrupt the
overall glass structure too much.

00:21:28.698 --> 00:21:30.060
(gentle music)

00:21:30.060 --> 00:21:31.440
Now that he had the composition,

00:21:31.440 --> 00:21:33.750
Maurer needed to tackle
that second problem.

00:21:33.750 --> 00:21:35.190
How do you make a tiny fiber

00:21:35.190 --> 00:21:38.190
with that perfect core and
cladding cross section?

00:21:38.190 --> 00:21:40.230
To help, he hired two more scientists,

00:21:40.230 --> 00:21:41.850
Peter Schultz, a ceramics engineer,

00:21:41.850 --> 00:21:44.400
and Donald Keck, a physicist.

00:21:44.400 --> 00:21:45.870
- Each time you find a problem,

00:21:45.870 --> 00:21:47.670
you put a development team on it

00:21:47.670 --> 00:21:50.910
and strike it down and make it happen.

00:21:50.910 --> 00:21:52.050
- To understand their technique,

00:21:52.050 --> 00:21:53.820
I went to the Bay Area Glass Institute

00:21:53.820 --> 00:21:55.890
for some hands-on demos.

00:21:55.890 --> 00:21:57.030
Here, our instructor, Erin

00:21:57.030 --> 00:21:58.740
is taking a colored rod of glass

00:21:58.740 --> 00:22:01.890
and then dipping it in a
bowl of clear molten glass.

00:22:01.890 --> 00:22:05.113
In fiber optics, this large
cylinder is called a preform.

00:22:05.113 --> 00:22:06.090
(gentle music)

00:22:06.090 --> 00:22:07.320
We have two colors of glass here.

00:22:07.320 --> 00:22:09.993
We have a color core and a clear outside.

00:22:11.160 --> 00:22:12.780
If you want your inner rod to, say,

00:22:12.780 --> 00:22:15.000
be a quarter of the
diameter of your outer one,

00:22:15.000 --> 00:22:16.200
you just set that in the preform,

00:22:16.200 --> 00:22:17.910
and then all the length along your pole,

00:22:17.910 --> 00:22:19.920
the cross-section's going to be identical.

00:22:19.920 --> 00:22:21.210
And this is pretty similar to how

00:22:21.210 --> 00:22:23.400
hard candies with patterns are made.

00:22:23.400 --> 00:22:26.730
This sugar is an amorphous
solid, just like glass.

00:22:26.730 --> 00:22:29.613
So the process is the same,
just at a lower temperature.

00:22:30.930 --> 00:22:33.000
But there is one problem with this method.

00:22:33.000 --> 00:22:34.800
Resting glass on a surface like this

00:22:34.800 --> 00:22:37.650
introduces a ton of stress and impurities,

00:22:37.650 --> 00:22:41.160
but when I try and suspend
it in the air, well, it sags.

00:22:41.160 --> 00:22:42.690
- [Erin] But yeah, from all that movement,

00:22:42.690 --> 00:22:43.920
it did create stress,

00:22:43.920 --> 00:22:47.910
so now it's a curved piece of, of glass.

00:22:47.910 --> 00:22:49.890
- Thankfully, there's another option.

00:22:49.890 --> 00:22:52.530
Now, gravity is pulling our glass for us,

00:22:52.530 --> 00:22:54.637
and the surface section
is keeping it together.

00:22:54.637 --> 00:22:55.470
(light dramatic music)

00:22:55.470 --> 00:23:00.180
So now we get a much more
straight and consistent fiber.

00:23:00.180 --> 00:23:01.532
That's awesome.

00:23:01.532 --> 00:23:02.760
(dramatic music continues)

00:23:02.760 --> 00:23:04.380
- With their drawing technique sorted,

00:23:04.380 --> 00:23:06.840
the corning team could
tackle the final problem.

00:23:06.840 --> 00:23:08.940
How do you actually make the preform?

00:23:08.940 --> 00:23:10.050
Well, instead of starting with

00:23:10.050 --> 00:23:11.700
a core and adding the cladding,

00:23:11.700 --> 00:23:13.080
Schultz and Keck decided to start

00:23:13.080 --> 00:23:14.913
from the cladding and work in.

00:23:15.900 --> 00:23:18.000
They used the flame hydrolysis technique

00:23:18.000 --> 00:23:20.430
to make titanium dope silica powder,

00:23:20.430 --> 00:23:21.810
or soot as they called it.

00:23:21.810 --> 00:23:24.030
Next, they took a pure fused silica tube

00:23:24.030 --> 00:23:26.520
and tried spreading soot down the inside.

00:23:26.520 --> 00:23:27.840
If they could get an even layer,

00:23:27.840 --> 00:23:29.640
well, then they could
melt and collapse the tube

00:23:29.640 --> 00:23:32.760
into a solid rod, a perfect preform.

00:23:32.760 --> 00:23:34.740
But the soot collected at one end

00:23:34.740 --> 00:23:36.990
no matter how they sprayed it.

00:23:36.990 --> 00:23:38.850
- So one of the technicians

00:23:38.850 --> 00:23:41.880
grabbed a vacuum cleaner
from the corner of the room,

00:23:41.880 --> 00:23:44.730
and he held it up to the
backside of this tube.

00:23:44.730 --> 00:23:48.240
And it sucked a stream of
titania dope silica glass

00:23:48.240 --> 00:23:50.190
down through the center of the tube,

00:23:50.190 --> 00:23:53.358
coated it just like we'd hoped it would.

00:23:53.358 --> 00:23:55.500
- [Henry] The vacuum did
not survive that experiment,

00:23:55.500 --> 00:23:56.820
but they tinkered with a new setup

00:23:56.820 --> 00:23:59.277
until they got the gas flow just right.

00:23:59.277 --> 00:24:00.330
(striking dramatic music)

00:24:00.330 --> 00:24:02.310
All through the summer of 1969,

00:24:02.310 --> 00:24:04.590
their furnace burned "hotter than Hades."

00:24:04.590 --> 00:24:06.810
They drew kilometers of fiber from preform

00:24:06.810 --> 00:24:09.450
after preform, testing
various compositions,

00:24:09.450 --> 00:24:11.220
but none of it was good enough.

00:24:11.220 --> 00:24:13.680
Titanium was breaking
down in the high heat

00:24:13.680 --> 00:24:15.720
and absorbing too much light.

00:24:15.720 --> 00:24:17.070
They ran the finished fibers through

00:24:17.070 --> 00:24:19.320
a second heat treatment
in an oxygen environment

00:24:19.320 --> 00:24:21.873
before they finally
started seeing results.

00:24:23.085 --> 00:24:24.270
(light dramatic music)

00:24:24.270 --> 00:24:27.210
A year later, on July 22, 1970,

00:24:27.210 --> 00:24:29.190
a preform slipped against the furnace wall

00:24:29.190 --> 00:24:31.920
after Keck only got a few meters of fiber.

00:24:31.920 --> 00:24:33.630
The preform was ruined,

00:24:33.630 --> 00:24:36.570
but he decided to test
the short fiber anyway

00:24:36.570 --> 00:24:37.620
after its heat treatment.

00:24:37.620 --> 00:24:38.880
- When I slid it across

00:24:38.880 --> 00:24:41.940
and the laser light hit
the core of the fiber,

00:24:41.940 --> 00:24:45.360
all of a sudden I had another
beam coming back in my face.

00:24:45.360 --> 00:24:47.160
The core was brightly lit,

00:24:47.160 --> 00:24:50.520
and it took a while to
realize that the light

00:24:50.520 --> 00:24:52.950
was going all the way to
the other end of the fiber

00:24:52.950 --> 00:24:57.090
and reflecting off the far end
and coming back into my eye.

00:24:57.090 --> 00:25:00.000
- [Henry] The loss was
17 decibels a kilometer.

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:02.250
- The only word I could come up with

00:25:02.250 --> 00:25:06.930
for this auspicious moment
in history was "Whoopie".

00:25:06.930 --> 00:25:09.567
So that's what's in my
lab notebook, "Whoopie".

00:25:11.010 --> 00:25:13.020
- [Henry] But the fiber was
much shorter than usual,

00:25:13.020 --> 00:25:15.990
only 29 meters to their typical 200.

00:25:15.990 --> 00:25:19.080
He added a careful "must
remeasure" in his notebook.

00:25:19.080 --> 00:25:20.700
Team quickly remade the preform,

00:25:20.700 --> 00:25:21.780
but it took almost a month

00:25:21.780 --> 00:25:23.973
to get 210 meters of finished fiber.

00:25:25.568 --> 00:25:27.090
It was finally done by late afternoon

00:25:27.090 --> 00:25:28.910
of Friday, August 21st.

00:25:28.910 --> 00:25:30.510
It was a gorgeous summer day

00:25:30.510 --> 00:25:31.920
and almost everyone left early,

00:25:31.920 --> 00:25:33.570
but Keck didn't want to wait.

00:25:33.570 --> 00:25:34.860
The fiber was so fragile,

00:25:34.860 --> 00:25:35.940
small temperature changes

00:25:35.940 --> 00:25:37.560
might break it over the weekend.

00:25:37.560 --> 00:25:40.440
- It would break if you
looked at it crossways.

00:25:40.440 --> 00:25:41.580
- So alone in the lab,

00:25:41.580 --> 00:25:44.100
he anxiously measured the loss,

00:25:44.100 --> 00:25:46.788
16.9 decibels per kilometer.

00:25:46.788 --> 00:25:47.790
(dramatic music continues)

00:25:47.790 --> 00:25:51.990
- I said "QED, quod erat demonstrandum"

00:25:51.990 --> 00:25:55.440
There was no, (chuckles) no eureka.

00:25:55.440 --> 00:25:57.690
- [Henry] Maurer published
their results that fall.

00:25:57.690 --> 00:26:00.570
As Schultz described
it, no one believed it.

00:26:00.570 --> 00:26:02.220
And telephone companies all demanded

00:26:02.220 --> 00:26:04.110
to see the fibers firsthand,

00:26:04.110 --> 00:26:06.530
so Maurer hand carried a spool of fiber

00:26:06.530 --> 00:26:09.030
to all the big telephone laboratories.

00:26:09.030 --> 00:26:10.860
He would literally guard to make sure

00:26:10.860 --> 00:26:13.308
that they didn't snip
pieces off and keep them.

00:26:13.308 --> 00:26:14.490
(dramatic music continues)

00:26:14.490 --> 00:26:16.653
For two years, they refined their methods.

00:26:17.490 --> 00:26:18.900
And to make the fiber stronger,

00:26:18.900 --> 00:26:21.150
they switched to a germanium doping.

00:26:21.150 --> 00:26:23.700
Like titanium it easily swaps with silicon

00:26:23.700 --> 00:26:26.190
while still raising the refractive index.

00:26:26.190 --> 00:26:27.390
But unlike titanium,

00:26:27.390 --> 00:26:29.730
germanium fibers didn't
break down in the heat,

00:26:29.730 --> 00:26:31.860
so they could skip the
second heat treatment.

00:26:31.860 --> 00:26:33.840
When they drew their
first germanium fiber,

00:26:33.840 --> 00:26:36.060
the end lit up with the
light from the furnace.

00:26:36.060 --> 00:26:37.230
Even when it reached the bottom

00:26:37.230 --> 00:26:38.820
and wound up around a large drum,

00:26:38.820 --> 00:26:40.740
the fiber stayed bright.

00:26:40.740 --> 00:26:43.350
- And we kept winding
and winding and winding

00:26:43.350 --> 00:26:45.540
and thinking the light
was going to go out,

00:26:45.540 --> 00:26:47.013
and it never went out.

00:26:47.880 --> 00:26:50.280
- The loss was only four
decibels per kilometer

00:26:50.280 --> 00:26:52.050
with excellent strength.

00:26:52.050 --> 00:26:53.940
Keck considers that the very first

00:26:53.940 --> 00:26:56.073
truly practical low-loss fiber.

00:26:57.480 --> 00:27:02.070
By 1977, AT&amp;T trialed a fiber
system underneath Chicago.

00:27:02.070 --> 00:27:04.860
Two years later, a fiber team
from Japan was able to get

00:27:04.860 --> 00:27:08.250
a loss of just 0.2 decibels a kilometer,

00:27:08.250 --> 00:27:10.200
the standard up to today.

00:27:10.200 --> 00:27:12.030
Fiber networks continued to grow

00:27:12.030 --> 00:27:14.400
with the first transatlantic
fiber optic cable

00:27:14.400 --> 00:27:16.107
installed by 1988.

00:27:16.107 --> 00:27:18.030
And it transmitted those phone calls

00:27:18.030 --> 00:27:19.440
differently than in copper.

00:27:19.440 --> 00:27:21.120
The audio wave was split up

00:27:21.120 --> 00:27:23.040
or sampled into discrete values,

00:27:23.040 --> 00:27:24.450
and then converted into binary

00:27:24.450 --> 00:27:26.850
to get a long string of ones and zeros.

00:27:26.850 --> 00:27:29.820
Then a laser flashed on
for one and off for zero,

00:27:29.820 --> 00:27:32.430
280 million times a second.

00:27:32.430 --> 00:27:34.740
These pulses of light
traveled down the fiber,

00:27:34.740 --> 00:27:36.330
but that wasn't all.

00:27:36.330 --> 00:27:39.360
Multiple conversations could
now be weaved together,

00:27:39.360 --> 00:27:40.710
one bit from one conversation,

00:27:40.710 --> 00:27:42.780
then one from another and so on,

00:27:42.780 --> 00:27:45.450
slotting it together like a zipper.

00:27:45.450 --> 00:27:48.300
As long as the receiver knew
the timing of each conversation

00:27:48.300 --> 00:27:49.920
and the sampling rate, well, they could

00:27:49.920 --> 00:27:51.633
reconstruct the original waves.

00:27:53.577 --> 00:27:54.660
(dramatic music)

00:27:54.660 --> 00:27:56.760
So this first intercontinental cable

00:27:56.760 --> 00:27:59.730
could carry 40,000 simultaneous calls,

00:27:59.730 --> 00:28:02.190
10 times more than the
previous copper version.

00:28:02.190 --> 00:28:05.010
This insane capacity opened
up a whole new world,

00:28:05.010 --> 00:28:06.930
and with digital signals people realized

00:28:06.930 --> 00:28:08.880
that they weren't limited to phone calls.

00:28:08.880 --> 00:28:13.110
It was easier than ever to
send texts, images, videos.

00:28:13.110 --> 00:28:15.540
All building up to the internet,

00:28:15.540 --> 00:28:17.853
and since then demand has only increased.

00:28:18.780 --> 00:28:20.453
- This reel right here?

00:28:20.453 --> 00:28:21.750
(light upbeat music)

00:28:21.750 --> 00:28:23.130
- Yeah.

00:28:23.130 --> 00:28:25.950
- 50.477 meter kilometers,

00:28:25.950 --> 00:28:27.690
so 50 kilometers.

00:28:27.690 --> 00:28:29.520
So it's about 30 miles.

00:28:29.520 --> 00:28:30.690
- [Henry] That's crazy.

00:28:30.690 --> 00:28:32.310
Like how much is in this warehouse?

00:28:32.310 --> 00:28:33.690
- There's millions of kilometers here

00:28:33.690 --> 00:28:36.090
that we're pushing
through here every month.

00:28:36.090 --> 00:28:37.920
- [Henry] To see how
modern fibers are made,

00:28:37.920 --> 00:28:40.590
we visited Prysmian's
North Carolina factory.

00:28:40.590 --> 00:28:42.570
It all starts with the preform.

00:28:42.570 --> 00:28:44.430
Each pass of this microwave source

00:28:44.430 --> 00:28:46.890
ignites a mixture of gases into a plasma

00:28:46.890 --> 00:28:48.900
which deposits a thin layer of glass

00:28:48.900 --> 00:28:50.280
on the inside of the tube.

00:28:50.280 --> 00:28:51.630
- And you see when it turns purple,

00:28:51.630 --> 00:28:54.720
that's when we're making
a plasma with that gas.

00:28:54.720 --> 00:28:58.020
- So, how thick are these deposition legs?

00:28:58.020 --> 00:28:59.232
Like how many times are you doing this?

00:28:59.232 --> 00:29:01.283
How thick are you doing--
- Thousands of passes.

00:29:01.283 --> 00:29:02.116
- [Henry] "Thousands of passes."

00:29:02.116 --> 00:29:04.050
- Several thousand times it's doing this.

00:29:04.050 --> 00:29:05.760
Every time we may go back and forth,

00:29:05.760 --> 00:29:08.175
we could change the composition
of that if we wanted to.

00:29:08.175 --> 00:29:09.360
- [Henry] Oh, okay.

00:29:09.360 --> 00:29:10.920
After all the inner layers are done,

00:29:10.920 --> 00:29:12.360
the tube has a thicker wall,

00:29:12.360 --> 00:29:14.250
but there's still a gap down the middle.

00:29:14.250 --> 00:29:16.890
- So we want to collapse
that channel upon itself.

00:29:16.890 --> 00:29:18.540
So what we do is we heat it up.

00:29:18.540 --> 00:29:20.880
We have a furnace that
goes back and forth,

00:29:20.880 --> 00:29:22.260
and just via surface tension,

00:29:22.260 --> 00:29:23.910
it collapses upon itself.

00:29:23.910 --> 00:29:24.840
- Interesting, I would've thought

00:29:24.840 --> 00:29:25.827
maybe you'd like pull it, or just--

00:29:25.827 --> 00:29:27.480
- [Sujeet] And I asked
this exact question.

00:29:27.480 --> 00:29:29.437
I said, "Are you pulling a vacuum in there

00:29:29.437 --> 00:29:30.720
"or what's making that thing shrink?"

00:29:30.720 --> 00:29:32.400
- Exactly.
- It's really just

00:29:32.400 --> 00:29:34.650
a glass phenomenon where the molecules

00:29:34.650 --> 00:29:36.060
are trying to pull together.

00:29:36.060 --> 00:29:38.310
You heat it up, you'll lower
the viscosity of the glass.

00:29:38.310 --> 00:29:39.750
It just... (exhales)

00:29:39.750 --> 00:29:41.790
- [Henry] Now they need to
add more silica on the outside

00:29:41.790 --> 00:29:44.010
to get the preform to
the right proportions.

00:29:44.010 --> 00:29:45.150
The core is very small,

00:29:45.150 --> 00:29:47.820
just 7.2% of the total diameter.

00:29:47.820 --> 00:29:50.280
- And you have a several thousand C

00:29:50.280 --> 00:29:51.870
plume of plasma right there.

00:29:51.870 --> 00:29:55.080
There's grains of silica
that's flowing into that plume

00:29:55.080 --> 00:29:57.090
and effectively melting it

00:29:57.090 --> 00:29:58.880
and spraying it onto that rod.

00:29:58.880 --> 00:29:59.820
- [Henry] Wow.
(steam hissing)

00:29:59.820 --> 00:30:03.150
- That plasma torch is
approximately three times

00:30:03.150 --> 00:30:05.473
as hot as the surface of the sun.

00:30:05.473 --> 00:30:06.393
- Plasma always blows my mind.

00:30:06.393 --> 00:30:08.100
This is absurd.

00:30:08.100 --> 00:30:09.180
Once the preform's done,

00:30:09.180 --> 00:30:10.770
it goes to the top of the draw tower,

00:30:10.770 --> 00:30:13.233
seven stories up to start making fiber.

00:30:15.020 --> 00:30:16.080
(steam hissing)

00:30:16.080 --> 00:30:19.050
It really looks like some sort
of steampunk thing, you know?

00:30:19.050 --> 00:30:22.139
All these coils and then
this hot glass going down.

00:30:22.139 --> 00:30:23.520
(gentle dramatic music)

00:30:23.520 --> 00:30:25.620
The bottom of the preform
is tapered to a point

00:30:25.620 --> 00:30:28.350
and heated to over 2,000 degrees Celsius.

00:30:28.350 --> 00:30:30.550
It's like there's some
heat coming off this.

00:30:31.500 --> 00:30:35.520
The end of the taper slowly
drops down to the floor below.

00:30:35.520 --> 00:30:38.183
- [Man] We call it a gob,
but it looks like a teardrop.

00:30:39.300 --> 00:30:40.470
- The fiber is a little thick,

00:30:40.470 --> 00:30:42.060
so it'll be thinned down to the final

00:30:42.060 --> 00:30:45.389
125 microns before it
passes to the next level.

00:30:45.389 --> 00:30:48.403
(gentle dramatic music)

00:30:48.403 --> 00:30:51.055
It's like a little flashlight coming down.

00:30:51.055 --> 00:30:53.805
(glass clinking)

00:30:57.523 --> 00:30:59.323
I didn't expect it to be so violent.

00:31:00.602 --> 00:31:02.910
(gentle dramatic music)

00:31:02.910 --> 00:31:04.680
If the drawing,

00:31:04.680 --> 00:31:08.310
like the pull down to that final diameter

00:31:08.310 --> 00:31:10.560
happens in such a short amount of...

00:31:10.560 --> 00:31:11.393
- In a few meters, let's call it.

00:31:11.393 --> 00:31:12.810
- A few meters.

00:31:12.810 --> 00:31:16.050
Then why do you need six more floors?

00:31:16.050 --> 00:31:17.190
- You got to cool the glass

00:31:17.190 --> 00:31:18.900
before you can apply the coating.

00:31:18.900 --> 00:31:21.000
And if you cool the glass too quickly,

00:31:21.000 --> 00:31:22.860
it'll have residual stress

00:31:22.860 --> 00:31:25.972
and then have optical loss attenuation.

00:31:25.972 --> 00:31:28.260
(glass clattering)

00:31:28.260 --> 00:31:29.580
- Once the fibers are cool enough,

00:31:29.580 --> 00:31:32.490
they are dipped in two
protective plastic coatings.

00:31:32.490 --> 00:31:33.750
At the bottom of the draw tower,

00:31:33.750 --> 00:31:36.330
the fiber is wound onto massive spools,

00:31:36.330 --> 00:31:38.670
each capable of carrying
hundreds of kilometers

00:31:38.670 --> 00:31:40.444
at over 50 meters a second.

00:31:40.444 --> 00:31:41.277
Okay.

00:31:42.750 --> 00:31:45.720
Next, the finished fiber is
tested for loss and strength.

00:31:45.720 --> 00:31:48.720
Fiber is stronger than a steel
wire of the same thickness.

00:31:48.720 --> 00:31:50.310
I guess if I try to do it like...

00:31:50.310 --> 00:31:51.709
- [Sujeet] If you just
try to do it like that,

00:31:51.709 --> 00:31:52.738
it's really hard to break.

00:31:52.738 --> 00:31:53.622
- First of all, it just
slides down my hand.

00:31:53.622 --> 00:31:55.913
- [Sujeet] And that's a glass
fiber with two coatings on it.

00:31:58.317 --> 00:32:01.140
- Ah, it's just hurting my hand.

00:32:01.140 --> 00:32:02.640
Oh, my god.

00:32:02.640 --> 00:32:03.630
Yeah, not easy.

00:32:03.630 --> 00:32:04.530
Definitely not easy.

00:32:04.530 --> 00:32:05.640
Ow.

00:32:05.640 --> 00:32:08.280
But it's weak to sharp turns.
- [Sujeet] Tie it in a knot.

00:32:08.280 --> 00:32:10.860
- Tie it in a knot.
- And watch what happens

00:32:10.860 --> 00:32:14.880
and see how much you can
get before it breaks.

00:32:14.880 --> 00:32:16.620
(gentle dramatic music)

00:32:16.620 --> 00:32:19.590
Yeah, because the angle's
so great, you can break it.

00:32:19.590 --> 00:32:21.360
- But after testing, fiber is bundled

00:32:21.360 --> 00:32:22.740
and wrapped into thicker cables

00:32:22.740 --> 00:32:25.350
so that it's harder for
them to knot and break.

00:32:25.350 --> 00:32:27.630
These cables will join the
more than eight billion

00:32:27.630 --> 00:32:29.790
kilometers of fiber already installed

00:32:29.790 --> 00:32:31.650
that carry information around the world,

00:32:31.650 --> 00:32:35.040
powering the internet and
letting you watch this video.

00:32:35.040 --> 00:32:37.320
But some experts argue
that even with fiber,

00:32:37.320 --> 00:32:38.790
the internet would not be possible

00:32:38.790 --> 00:32:41.040
without another key invention.

00:32:41.040 --> 00:32:43.170
The amplifier, that's what boosts signals

00:32:43.170 --> 00:32:44.610
across long stretches,

00:32:44.610 --> 00:32:47.943
and it revolutionized fiber
capacity on land and sea.

00:32:48.780 --> 00:32:51.120
So if you'd like to see a
video about submarine cables

00:32:51.120 --> 00:32:54.345
and fiber amplifiers, well,
let us know in the comments.

00:32:54.345 --> 00:32:55.410
(quiet dramatic music)

00:32:55.410 --> 00:32:58.590
As for Kao, his dream went
further than he ever thought.

00:32:58.590 --> 00:33:02.010
When he received the Nobel
Prize in 2009 for his work,

00:33:02.010 --> 00:33:04.387
he said, "The information
revolution that started

00:33:04.387 --> 00:33:06.217
"in the 1990s could not have happened

00:33:06.217 --> 00:33:07.687
"without optical fibers.

00:33:07.687 --> 00:33:10.147
"It is beyond the dreams of
the first serious concept

00:33:10.147 --> 00:33:12.667
"of optical communication in 1966,

00:33:12.667 --> 00:33:15.237
"when even one gigahertz was only a hope."

00:33:16.620 --> 00:33:18.630
For me, one of the things
I kept on coming back to

00:33:18.630 --> 00:33:22.350
in this story is that Kao
was not a material scientist.

00:33:22.350 --> 00:33:24.090
He was an electrical engineer.

00:33:24.090 --> 00:33:26.040
And it was only because he was an outsider

00:33:26.040 --> 00:33:27.750
that he was able to ask the questions

00:33:27.750 --> 00:33:29.190
that nobody was asking.

00:33:29.190 --> 00:33:31.650
He was able to push the status quo,

00:33:31.650 --> 00:33:32.730
but he was not the only one.

00:33:32.730 --> 00:33:34.560
Maurer's team were also working

00:33:34.560 --> 00:33:36.600
with a very unintuitive idea

00:33:36.600 --> 00:33:38.610
that by making something seemingly worse,

00:33:38.610 --> 00:33:40.560
they actually were making it better.

00:33:40.560 --> 00:33:42.847
As Kao said, "Who knows
what other forward thinkers

00:33:42.847 --> 00:33:44.850
"will dream of in the future?"

00:33:44.850 --> 00:33:46.680
Granted, he was talking about bandwidth,

00:33:46.680 --> 00:33:48.540
but I think it applies anywhere

00:33:48.540 --> 00:33:50.070
where people are able to question

00:33:50.070 --> 00:33:51.840
why things are the way they are

00:33:51.840 --> 00:33:54.133
and whether that's really the limit.

00:33:54.133 --> 00:33:57.120
(electric chirping)

00:33:57.120 --> 00:33:57.953
One last thing.

00:33:57.953 --> 00:33:59.250
These videos are only possible

00:33:59.250 --> 00:34:00.660
because of the incredible work done

00:34:00.660 --> 00:34:02.400
by our team behind the scenes.

00:34:02.400 --> 00:34:05.520
We have world-class editors,
animators, researchers,

00:34:05.520 --> 00:34:07.980
and writers that make
these videos possible.

00:34:07.980 --> 00:34:09.690
We'd love to shine more
of a light on their work,

00:34:09.690 --> 00:34:10.773
so we organized a Q&amp;A

00:34:10.773 --> 00:34:12.630
with the writer of today's video, Sulli.

00:34:12.630 --> 00:34:14.070
She discusses the fascinating things

00:34:14.070 --> 00:34:15.810
she learned while researching this topic

00:34:15.810 --> 00:34:17.400
and things that didn't make the cut,

00:34:17.400 --> 00:34:18.780
but that are really interesting.

00:34:18.780 --> 00:34:20.460
So if you want to learn more about that,

00:34:20.460 --> 00:34:22.440
you can sign up for our
newsletter in the description

00:34:22.440 --> 00:34:24.718
and you can find her Q&amp;A right there.

00:34:24.718 --> 00:34:27.885
